as storytellers we are always searching  for incredible sources of pressure to  put on our character you're working on a  war game on a military on a shooter  you're kind of go to Guinea source of  pressure is an enemy that is trying to  shoot at you and then within a few  warfare having a lot of the missions  take place outside of our breathable  atmosphere that just puts even more  pressure on you now you have an  environment that's trying to kill you as  well you know if you can't breathe  you don't know which way is up if you  don't have normal gravity these are all  things that are conspiring against you  as a player conspiring against your  squad and it just adds to that pressure  and we're always looking for those  ultimate sources of pressure because  when you have those ultimate sources of  pressure all the niceties go out the  window and you really know what makes a  person tick and revealing that true  nature of these people that's  storytelling in a nutshell and that's  the kind of stuff that we look for as  sort of as a way to educate ourselves  going through our own lives there are  three studios taking turns on the annual  Call of Duty franchise two years ago it  was sledgehammer games with an advanced  warfare  last year it was Treyarch with black ops  3 and now in 2016 it's time for Infinity  Ward the studio that started the  franchise in the first place  to take its turn with the ubiquitous  shooter series and although Call of Duty  draws millions of players with its  fast-paced progression heavy multiplayer  modes Infinity Ward looked elsewhere  when developing infinite warfare it  looked to the single-player campaign not  just its structure and set peace moments  but also its story and how that story is  told  and in an effort to set this new  narrative apart Infinity Ward took to  outer space  first and foremost from a narrative  standpoint we wanted to tell a classic  war story in the great tradition  hopefully of a lot of the war stories  that have come before us in books and  movies and TV and we wanted to have  something that was sort of transcended  time or transcendent setting  so despite the stories new frontier it  is in many ways a return to Call of  Duty's World War two roots it's a story  of black and white good versus evil it's  the story about a massive sneak attack  and the resulting war that changes its  participants over time in a lot of  really well-received war stories the  story really centers on you and your  squad and what you guys are going  through and how you have each other's  backs and how the pressures of the war  and the enemy hunting you can reveal  things about your true nature  it's a stress  it's a pressure that's put on your guys  and and we think that's a very  interesting thing to to reveal well sure  saw in the drone captain's orders that  somebody and it's Bob not drone call me  Sara I work for a living  check your pop captain for a throw  Maddie a look well we wanted to tell a  story about the burden of leadership and  we wanted to tell a story about people  that cared about each other you know one  of the things that we recognized from  spending time and from some of them you  know we've been inspired by movies like  Black Hawk Down Saving Private Ryan  shows like Band of Brothers and one of  the takeaways as storytellers and and as  an actor as a writer are these  remarkable relationships  we're trying to be grounded we're trying  to be in this world what would really  happen to these people even though this  is a this is a future setting even  though this is a thing that's like you  know we don't really know what that's  gonna look like but we're we're saying  this is what it looks like and if that's  what it looks like and we're still  people how do these people react and how  do these moments occur and everything so  the bomb even in the bomb vest we were  trying to make it like this is  believable this is a thing that could  happen we're gonna reinforce these  characters and have these moments in a  level rather than in between levels and  we're gonna think about this arc of  character development and how that  changes over the course of the game more  than we've thought about that in the  past  members of the new Infinity Ward have  worked on several iconic series tomb  raider uncharted in the Last of Us to  name a few  Kurosaki himself came from Naughty Dog a  studio known for its storytelling  prowess he's helped shaped stories about  roguish treasure hunters and post  apocalyptic relationships and as was the  case with those stories the team working  on infinite warfare wants to morph not  just its narrative but how that  narrative affects the gameplay a big  tenant of this game was we wanted to  give the players some choices we wanted  to give the players mission choices and  we wanted to feel that you were leading  the charge so we created this character  of Reyes who is the captain of the  retribution you are the captain of your  own carrier and yeah that was a  challenge it was a challenge not having  the sort of fallback of a more senior  leader telling you where to go and what  to do ground assault captains places on  the bridge not this captain I think one  of the things that came out of ghosts  and some and some cod games we've built  over the past was this idea of playing a  strong leadership role rather than being  kind of one of the junior guys in the  crew a real big focus for us is making  sure that the player is in sync  emotionally with the protagonists  disrespected you don't let him under  your skin and the more successfully you  can do that the more bought into the  story and bought into the narrative and  bought into the characters the player  feels and the more closeness he feels  and the more care the player feels and  therefore again you have these set  pieces and the more emotional and more  kind of like impactful they are try and  stop me sir  in the beginning there was a lot of  prototyping going on around different  ideas and concepts and stuff like this  and a prototype that we did here that  they were working on that was really  about that kind of transition moment in  the in the game now the first time you  kind of take off from Geneva and that  launch sequence and that transition up  to that first big space battle that it's  kind of was kind of the essence of that  kind of cool idea the idea that you  could transition from something so  familiar like a like a storm the hill a  charged the hill moment a very kind of  mana warfare feeling moment through this  very kind of cinematic transition and up  and then into something that was totally  new and different there are going to be  moments of bombast they're gonna be huge  things that happen to these characters  and then they're also going to be synced  with things that seem smaller intimate  and more emotional and the the  juxtaposition between those two things  we feel that makes it a little bit more  impactful captain's dude used to get his  men on the lodge staff sergeant always a  tenant  always  if Infinity Ward succeeds the result  will be a mash-up of World War two  camaraderie spectacular blockbuster set  pieces and personal player driven  moments all outside the confines of  Earth's atmosphere a story with massive  scope but intimate focus Infinity Ward  was there right at Call of Duty's  inception in 2003 Infinity Ward was  there when the shooter entered modern  times in 2007 but in recent years the  Studios played somewhat of a tertiary  role to Treyarch and sledgehammer games  yet with infinite warfare with its focus  on narrative player choice in a bold  style of campaign storytelling Infinity  Ward hopes once again to take hold of  the franchise it began all those years  ago
 

